# Django coding style

Narrows `python`'s coding style to Django-project conventions. Applies to
`*.py` files inside a Django project; non-Django Python in the same
repository (a standalone script, a shared library package with no Django
import) still comes from `python`'s own coding-style rule, not this file.

## App and project layout

- One Django "app" per cohesive domain concern (`billing/`, `accounts/`),
  each with its own `models.py`/`views.py`/`urls.py`/`migrations/` — do not
  grow a single app into an unrelated dumping ground once it outgrows one
  clear responsibility.
- Name an app's `urls.py` route names with the app as a namespace prefix
  (`billing:invoice-detail`) and reverse them with `reverse()`/`{% url %}`,
  never a hand-built path string, so a route can move without breaking every
  caller.
- Keep `settings.py` split by environment (`settings/base.py` +
  `dev.py`/`prod.py`, or `django-environ`/`django-configurations`) when the
  project already uses that layout; match the existing convention rather
  than introducing a second settings-splitting scheme.

## Models

- Give every model field an explicit `verbose_name`/`help_text` when the
  field name alone would not be self-explanatory to someone using the admin
  or a generated form.
- Use `related_name` on a `ForeignKey`/`ManyToManyField`/`OneToOneField`
  whenever the default (`<model>_set`) would collide or read unclearly at
  the call site; pick a name that reads naturally from the related side
  (`author.books`, not `author.book_set`).
- Put field-level defaults and choices near the field (`choices=Status.choices`
  with a `TextChoices`/`IntegerChoices` enum), not a bare tuple of literal
  strings repeated at each call site.
- Override `save()`/add a `clean()` only for invariants that must hold for
  every write path (including the admin and shell); a validation rule that
  only applies to one form belongs on that `Form`, not the model.

## Views and URLs

- Prefer a class-based view (`ListView`, `DetailView`, `CreateView`, or a
  DRF `APIView`/`ViewSet` when the project uses Django REST Framework) for
  the framework's standard CRUD shapes; reach for a function-based view when
  the logic does not map cleanly onto a generic view's hooks — current
  Django guidance treats CBVs and FBVs as equally supported, not one as
  deprecated.
- Keep business logic out of the view body: a view coordinates
  request/response and delegates the actual work to a model method, a form's
  `clean`/`save`, or a service function — a view that inlines several
  unrelated queries and branches is a sign the logic belongs elsewhere.
- Name URL patterns and match the project's existing `path()`/`re_path()`
  convention; prefer `path()` with converters (`<int:pk>`) over `re_path()`
  unless the match genuinely needs a regex.

## Forms and serializers

- Validate with a `Form`/`ModelForm` (or DRF `Serializer`/`ModelSerializer`)
  `clean_<field>`/`clean()`/`validate_<field>`/`validate()` method, not
  ad-hoc validation scattered in the view after the fact.
- Use `ModelForm`/`ModelSerializer` with an explicit `fields = [...]` list
  (never `fields = "__all__"` on a form or serializer that accepts
  user-submitted data) so a new model field is not silently exposed for
  mass-assignment until it is deliberately added.

## Imports and structure

- Follow `python`'s stdlib/third-party/local import grouping; within
  "third-party", Django's own imports (`django.db`, `django.http`, ...)
  commonly sort before other third-party packages alphabetically — match
  what the project's `ruff`/`isort` config already produces rather than
  hand-ordering.
- Import from `django.conf.settings`, never re-read environment variables
  directly in application code that already has a settings value for it.
